# Section 3 - Behavioral specifications

This document is the behavioral contract for every module in the Section 2
partition. It is written before RTL implementation and follows the Section 3
requirements from `HW1-1.pdf`: reset type and values, bit widths and signedness,
default behavior, invalid-input behavior, complete FSM state behavior, and
cycle-by-cycle timing are stated explicitly.

The module headers and architectural responsibilities remain in
`partitioning.md_02` and the individual Section 2 blueprint files.

## 1. Global conventions

### 1.1 Clock, reset, and cycle terminology

- All sequential modules use a positive-edge clock named `clk`.
- `rst` is synchronous and active high. A module changes to its reset state
  only on a rising edge for which `rst == 1`.
- A request sampled on rising edge `N` is said to be accepted at cycle `N`.
- Unless a module states another latency, a registered response visible after
  the next rising edge is the response at cycle `N + 1`.
- Reset has priority over every request, valid, ready, and state transition.
- A one-cycle pulse is high for exactly one clock interval between two rising
  edges.

### 1.2 Data representation

- Every FP16 value is a 16-bit `logic [15:0]` bit pattern representing IEEE-754
  binary16. The ports are not signed integers; arithmetic modules interpret
  the bit patterns as FP16.
- Addresses, indices, counts, masks, and control signals are unsigned logic
  vectors.
- Supplied arithmetic inputs are finite FP16 values. The design need not
  generate IEEE-754 NaNs or infinities.
- Every multiplication is rounded to FP16 before it is presented as a product.
- Every addition is rounded to FP16 before the accumulator stores the result.
- No FP32 accumulator, fused multiply-add, reduction tree, or reordering is
  permitted.

### 1.3 Validity and ignored inputs

- A value is consumed only in a cycle in which its corresponding `valid` is
  high and the receiving interface is ready, when a ready signal exists.
- Data and indices on an invalid cycle are ignored and may be driven to zero
  by the producer for deterministic simulation.
- A request received while a stateful unit is busy is ignored unless the
  module's specific section says that it is queued. No module queues requests
  implicitly.
- Undefined addresses and indices never alter architectural state. Their
  response is invalid and their data outputs are zero unless otherwise stated.

## 2. `input_memory`

### 2.1 Stored contents and address interpretation

The memory contains exactly 784 FP16 pixels, in the order of
`test_image.mem`. A seven-pixel group request uses:

```text
pixel_index = 7 * group_addr + lane
group_addr  = 0 .. 111
lane        = 0 .. 6
```

Every legal group has seven valid lanes because 784 is divisible by seven.

### 2.2 Sequential behavior

| Condition at rising edge | State/output after the edge |
|---|---|
| `rst == 1` | `group_valid=0`, `lane_valid=0`, and all seven outputs are `16'h0000`. Any pending read is discarded. |
| `rst == 0`, `read_req=1`, `group_addr <= 111` | The request is registered. One cycle later, `group_valid=1`, all seven outputs contain the requested pixels, and `lane_valid=7'b1111111`. |
| `rst == 0`, `read_req=0` | No new request is accepted. `group_valid=0` on the next cycle. |
| `group_addr >= 112` | The request is invalid and has no architectural effect. The response is `group_valid=0`, `lane_valid=0`, and all data outputs zero. |

Consecutive legal requests are returned in order, one response per cycle after
the one-cycle read latency.

## 3. `weight_sram`

### 3.1 Stored contents and address interpretation

The logical row address is 10 bits and maps as follows:

| Row address | Logical contents |
|---:|---|
| `0 .. 895` | Seven consecutive values from flattened `fc1_weights.mem` |
| `896 .. 907` | Seven consecutive physical positions from flattened `fc2_weights.mem` |
| `908 .. 1023` | Undefined/reserved; never a legal request |

For a legal row, output lanes are ordered lane 0 through lane 6. The output
row is 112 bits conceptually, represented by seven registered 16-bit outputs.

### 3.2 Seven-cycle latency

If `read_req=1` and `row_addr` is legal at edge `N`, the SRAM accepts the
request. Exactly seven rising edges later, at edge `N+7`, it asserts
`row_valid=1` for one cycle and presents the corresponding seven registered
weights and `lane_valid`. Requests accepted on consecutive edges produce
responses on consecutive edges in the same order. Reset flushes all pending
requests.

### 3.3 Reset and invalid requests

| Condition | Required behavior |
|---|---|
| `rst=1` at an edge | Clear the request pipeline, clear `row_valid` and `lane_valid`, and drive all weight outputs to zero. |
| Legal request | Accept it even if another legal request was accepted on the preceding cycle; the seven-cycle pipeline preserves order. |
| `row_addr >= 908` | Do not create a valid response. Return zero data with `row_valid=0` if a response slot is generated. |
| Padding lane | `lane_valid[k]=0` means that lane is not a weight and must not create a multiplier product. |

The actual `fc2_weights.mem` contains 80 values. Therefore row 907 contains
three valid values in lanes 0..2 and four padding positions in lanes 3..6;
its physical `lane_valid` is `7'b0000111`. This decision is used throughout
the remaining specification.

For a second-layer digit `d` and hidden index `h`, the physical location is:

```text
flat_index = 8 * d + h
row_addr   = 896 + floor(flat_index / 7)
row_lane   = flat_index % 7
```

The SRAM returns physical rows. The controller captures and selects the
required lanes into a logical group before asserting `multiplier_start`; the
multiplier never pairs a weight from one digit with an input from another.

## 4. `bias_memory`

### 4.1 Address map

The memory contains exactly 18 FP16 values:

```text
bias_addr 0 .. 7   -> fc1_bias[n], n = 0 .. 7
bias_addr 8 .. 17  -> fc2_bias[d], d = 0 .. 9
```

### 4.2 Behavior

A legal `read_req` sampled at edge `N` returns `bias_valid=1` and the selected
`bias_out` at edge `N+1`. `bias_valid` is a one-cycle pulse. Requests can be
accepted on consecutive cycles and responses remain ordered.

Reset drives `bias_valid=0` and `bias_out=16'h0000`. Addresses 18 through 31
are invalid: they produce no valid response and never change memory contents.

## 5. `fp16_multiplier`

### 5.1 Group capture

`start_group` is accepted only when `busy=0` and `rst=0`. At acceptance, the
module captures all seven input operands, all seven weight operands, and the
seven-bit `lane_valid` mask. A `start_group` while busy is ignored; the
currently active group is never overwritten.

### 5.2 Product schedule

The captured group is visited in fixed lane order:

| Active cycle after acceptance | `product_index` | Required result |
|---:|---:|---|
| 0 | 0 | Product 0 if `lane_valid[0]=1`; otherwise invalid/zero |
| 1 | 1 | Product 1 if `lane_valid[1]=1`; otherwise invalid/zero |
| 2 | 2 | Product 2 if `lane_valid[2]=1`; otherwise invalid/zero |
| 3 | 3 | Product 3 if `lane_valid[3]=1`; otherwise invalid/zero |
| 4 | 4 | Product 4 if `lane_valid[4]=1`; otherwise invalid/zero |
| 5 | 5 | Product 5 if `lane_valid[5]=1`; otherwise invalid/zero |
| 6 | 6 | Product 6 if `lane_valid[6]=1`; otherwise invalid/zero; `group_done=1` |

On a valid lane, the output is exactly `FP16(weight[k] * input[k])`,
`product_valid=1`, and `product_out` is the rounded FP16 result. On an invalid
lane, `product_valid=0` and `product_out=16'h0000`; no multiplication result
is architecturally produced. `group_done` is asserted only for the final
lane-position cycle, even when lane 6 is invalid.

`busy` is high from group acceptance through the cycle carrying
`group_done`. It clears at the following rising edge. Reset aborts the group,
clears `busy`, `product_valid`, `group_done`, `product_out`, and
`product_index`.

## 6. `ping_pong_buffer`

### 6.1 Half states

Each seven-entry half is always in exactly one of these states:

| State | Meaning | Legal transition |
|---|---|---|
| `EMPTY` | No unread products are stored. | `product_valid && write_ready` writes the first product and enters `WRITING`. |
| `WRITING` | Current multiplier group is being collected. | Valid products append in order. `product_last` closes the group and enters `FULL`. |
| `FULL` | A complete group is waiting for the accumulator. | `consume_start` enters `READING`; otherwise it remains `FULL`. |
| `READING` | Stored products are offered in order. | A final accepted product enters `EMPTY`; otherwise it remains `READING`. |

The two halves may be in different states simultaneously. A half is never
written while it is `FULL` or `READING`, and a half is never read while it is
`EMPTY` or `WRITING`.

### 6.2 Write behavior

If `product_valid=1` and `write_ready=1`, `product_in` is written to the next
free position. If `product_valid=1` and `write_ready=0`, the producer must
hold the product and its valid indication; the buffer does not silently drop
it. `product_last` closes the group after all valid products in that group
have been counted. Invalid multiplier positions are not written.

The controller must never start a group that would require more than seven
valid products in one half.

### 6.3 Read behavior

`consume_start` is accepted only for a `FULL` half. While reading, the buffer
holds `consume_data` and `consume_last` stable until
`consume_valid && consume_ready` is true. `consume_last=1` accompanies the
last stored valid product, not an invalid/padding position. After that product
is accepted, the half becomes `EMPTY` and can eventually be selected for
writing.

Reset returns both halves to `EMPTY`, clears all valid/full/empty indicators,
and drives all data/status outputs inactive.

## 7. `fp16_accumulator`

### 7.1 Neuron initialization

`neuron_start` is accepted only while `busy=0`. It loads `bias_in` directly
into the FP16 accumulator; the bias is not added through a product cycle.
The first accepted product is added to that bias.

If `neuron_start` is asserted while busy, it is ignored. Reset clears the
accumulator, `busy`, `result_valid`, and `result_out`.

### 7.2 Product acceptance and ordered fold

When `product_valid && product_ready` is high, the module performs exactly one
FP16 addition:

```text
new_acc = FP16(old_acc + product_in)
```

`group_last` marks the end of one seven-position buffer group but does not
alter the accumulator. Products in later groups continue the same left fold.
`product_valid` while `product_ready=0` is not consumed; the buffer must hold
the product stable.

When `neuron_last=1` accompanies the final accepted product, `result_out`
receives the *updated* sum and `result_valid` pulses for one cycle. The result
must include that final product. `busy` clears after completion.

## 8. `layer2_register_file`

### 8.1 Storage and writes

The ten entries reset to `16'h0000`. Hidden writes are accepted only when
`hidden_write_en=1` and `hidden_write_addr` is 0 through 7. Addresses 8 and 9
are reserved and a write to them is ignored. A valid write takes effect on the
accepting rising edge.

### 8.2 Group reads

A legal `group_read_req` is registered and returns one cycle later:

| `group_addr` | Lane contents | `lane_valid` |
|---:|---|---|
| 0 | hidden[0] through hidden[6] | `7'b1111111` |
| 1 | hidden[7] in lane 0; lanes 1 through 6 unused | `7'b0000001` |

`group_valid=1` only for the response cycle. An invalid group address cannot
occur with the one-bit port; reset clears `group_valid`, `lane_valid`, and all
entries. The hidden values must not be overwritten while any second-layer
neuron still needs them.

## 9. `relu`

`relu` is combinational and has no state, clock, or reset. For a valid input:

| `data_in[15]` | Meaning for finite FP16 input | `data_out` |
|---:|---|---|
| 0 | Positive value or positive zero | Exact unchanged 16-bit input |
| 1 | Negative value or negative zero | `16'h0000` |

`valid_out` is exactly `valid_in`. When `valid_in=0`, `data_out` is driven to
`16'h0000` for deterministic simulation and must be ignored by storage. This
normalizes negative zero to positive zero.

## 10. `argmax`

### 10.1 Collection states

| State | Behavior | Transition |
|---|---|---|
| `IDLE` | `busy=0`, waiting for `start`; previous outputs remain stable until a new start or reset. | Legal `start` -> clear transaction storage and enter `COLLECT`. |
| `COLLECT` | `busy=1`; accept valid logits with indices 0..9 and compare them. | Valid `logit_last` for the final logit -> store result, pulse `done`, enter `IDLE`. |
| `DONE` | Not a persistent state; `done` is a one-cycle registered pulse associated with completion. | Next cycle returns to `IDLE`. |

`start` while `COLLECT` is ignored. `logit_valid=0` causes no write or
comparison. A valid index 10 through 15 is invalid and is ignored. A
`logit_last` without a valid logit is ignored; the controller must assert it
with the final valid digit logit.

### 10.2 Comparison and tie behavior

The first accepted valid logit initializes the running maximum and prediction.
Each later valid logit replaces the running maximum only if it is strictly
greater than the current maximum. Equality never replaces the existing index,
so the lowest index wins a tie when logits arrive in digit order.

Each valid logit is also written to its corresponding `logitN_out` register.
After the final logit, all ten outputs and `prediction` remain stable until
the next `start` or reset. Reset clears all ten outputs, `prediction`,
`busy`, and `done`.

## 11. `controller_fsm`

### 11.1 Controller invariants

- `start` is accepted only in `IDLE`; a start while busy is ignored.
- `busy=1` in every state except `IDLE` and the completion return cycle.
- All output request pulses are low by default and are asserted only in the
  state that owns the request.
- A memory/datapath response that is not valid does not advance the FSM.
- All counters have explicit bounds: first-layer neuron 0..7, first-layer
  group 0..111, second-layer digit 0..9, and second-layer logical input
  positions 0..7.
- In the second layer, the controller owns non-arithmetic staging registers
  for physical SRAM rows. It never starts the multiplier until the selected
  lanes for one digit's logical group are assembled.

### 11.1a Controller status interface

The `controller_fsm` official header includes these status inputs in addition
to its clock, reset, start, operand, and datapath inputs:

| Input | Source | Sampling rule |
|---|---|---|
| `bias_valid` | `bias_memory.bias_valid` | Capture the bias only on its one-cycle response pulse. |
| `layer2_group_valid` | `layer2_register_file.group_valid` | Capture hidden operands only on the registered group response. |
| `buffer_full` | `ping_pong_buffer.buffer_full` | Assert `buffer_consume_start` only when an oldest full half is available. |
| `buffer_consume_valid` | `ping_pong_buffer.consume_valid` | Qualifies the buffered product and its last marker. |
| `buffer_consume_ready` | `fp16_accumulator.product_ready` | Indicates that the current buffered product is accepted. |

`buffer_consume_last` remains the buffer's last-product indicator. A group is
consumed, and the controller may advance its group counter, only when
`buffer_consume_valid && buffer_consume_ready && buffer_consume_last` is true
in the same cycle. This rule is required for partial groups and for stable
data under consumer back-pressure. These inputs do not add arithmetic or
change any module's responsibility.

### 11.2 Complete state list

| State | Main outputs | Advance condition | Next state |
|---|---|---|---|
| `IDLE` | `busy=0`; no requests | `start=1` | `FC1_BIAS_REQ` |
| `FC1_BIAS_REQ` | Request bias address `fc1_neuron` | Request pulse issued | `FC1_BIAS_WAIT` |
| `FC1_BIAS_WAIT` | Wait for `bias_valid` | Bias valid | `FC1_INPUT_REQ` |
| `FC1_INPUT_REQ` | Request input group `fc1_group` and corresponding weight row | Both requests issued | `FC1_OPERAND_WAIT` |
| `FC1_OPERAND_WAIT` | Hold addresses/control; no multiplier start | Input group and SRAM row valid and buffer writable | `FC1_MAC_START` |
| `FC1_MAC_START` | Pulse `multiplier_start`, pulse accumulator start only for the first group | Start pulse issued | `FC1_GROUP_WAIT` |
| `FC1_GROUP_WAIT` | Wait for multiplier/buffer/accumulator group completion | Accepted last product: `buffer_consume_valid && buffer_consume_ready && buffer_consume_last` | If `fc1_group==111`, `FC1_RESULT_WAIT`; else increment group and return to `FC1_INPUT_REQ` |
| `FC1_RESULT_WAIT` | Wait for `accumulator_result_valid` | Result valid | `FC1_RELU_STORE` |
| `FC1_RELU_STORE` | Enable hidden write at `fc1_neuron` | Write enabled | If neuron 7, `FC2_BIAS_REQ`; else increment neuron and go to `FC1_BIAS_REQ` |
| `FC2_BIAS_REQ` | Request bias address `8 + fc2_digit` | Request pulse issued | `FC2_BIAS_WAIT` |
| `FC2_BIAS_WAIT` | Wait for `bias_valid` | Bias valid | `FC2_GROUP_REQ` |
| `FC2_GROUP_REQ` | Request layer-two hidden group and the first required physical weight row | Request issued | `FC2_WEIGHT_GATHER` |
| `FC2_WEIGHT_GATHER` | Capture each required physical SRAM row and select lanes using `flat_index = 8*digit + hidden_index` | All lanes for the current logical group assembled | `FC2_OPERAND_WAIT` |
| `FC2_OPERAND_WAIT` | Wait for `layer2_group_valid` and an available buffer; hold the assembled logical weight group | Hidden response valid and writable | `FC2_MAC_START` |
| `FC2_MAC_START` | Pulse multiplier start and accumulator start | Start pulse issued | `FC2_GROUP_WAIT` |
| `FC2_GROUP_WAIT` | Wait for the seven-position group and accumulation | Accepted last product: `buffer_consume_valid && buffer_consume_ready && buffer_consume_last` | If final logical input, `FC2_RESULT_WAIT`; else request next group |
| `FC2_RESULT_WAIT` | Wait for final accumulator result | Result valid | `FC2_LOGIT_SEND` |
| `FC2_LOGIT_SEND` | Pulse argmax logit valid with current digit index; assert logit last for digit 9 | Pulse issued | If digit 9, `COMPLETE_WAIT`; else increment digit and return to `FC2_BIAS_REQ` |
| `COMPLETE_WAIT` | Hold outputs; no new transaction | `argmax_done` | `IDLE` with one-cycle `done` pulse |

`FC2_WEIGHT_GATHER` may issue more than one physical SRAM request for one
logical group because a digit's eight flattened weights can cross a physical
seven-value row boundary. It advances only after every selected lane has been
captured and invalid/padding lanes have been excluded.

### 11.3 Reset and default outputs

At reset, the FSM enters `IDLE`, clears all counters and request pulses, and
drives `busy=0`, `done=0`, and all addresses to zero. Any unlisted or illegal
state must recover to `IDLE` on the next rising edge with all request pulses
deasserted.

## 12. Resolved second-layer interpretation

The supplied `fc2_weights.mem` has 80 values, so physical row 907 has three
valid values and four padding positions. The design uses
`lane_valid = 7'b0000111` for that row.

The physical SRAM layout remains globally flattened, while the logical MAC
order remains per digit. The controller performs the explicit lane-selection
and regrouping step before each second-layer multiplier start:

```text
flat_index = 8 * digit + hidden_index
row_addr   = 896 + floor(flat_index / 7)
row_lane   = flat_index % 7
```

Each digit is evaluated as two logical groups: hidden indices 0..6, then
hidden index 7 with six invalid lanes. This preserves the reference model's
ordered FP16 MAC without changing the input files or pairing weights across
digits.
